<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  P09: pin the listeners to IPv4 only.

  Why this file exists. The official image ships
  /etc/clickhouse-server/config.d/docker_related_config.xml, which declares both
  <listen_host>::</listen_host> and <listen_host>0.0.0.0</listen_host>. On a
  kernel with IPv6 disabled, which is the case for the WSL2 kernel this lab runs
  on, every [::] bind fails with

      Listen [::]:9000 failed: ... EAI: Address family for hostname not supported

  and ClickHouse logs that at Warning level and carries on. The server then
  comes up with some listeners bound and some not, and the image's HTTP
  healthcheck on 8123 reports "healthy" while the native protocol on 9000
  refuses connections. Everything that speaks native, including
  clickhouse-client, P06's Makefile targets and this project's DDL init job,
  then fails against a container Compose is willing to call ready.

  That is P09 section 2 in miniature: service_healthy is a statement about a
  probe, not about the service. The probe in compose.dev.yaml is widened to
  cover both ports; this file removes the cause rather than only detecting it.

  replace="replace" is required. config.d files MERGE by default, so a second
  <listen_host> would append to the image's list and leave the failing [::]
  entry in place.

  The filename sorts after docker_related_config.xml deliberately: ClickHouse
  applies config.d in lexicographic order, so the "zz-" prefix is load-bearing.

  Note for anyone editing this comment: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a
  comment. ClickHouse refuses to start on a config it cannot parse and
  crash-loops, which is the correct behaviour and an easy own-goal.

  Re-pin for a host that does have IPv6: binding 0.0.0.0 only would then
  silently drop IPv6 clients, the same class of mistake in reverse.
-->
<clickhouse>
    <listen_host replace="replace">0.0.0.0</listen_host>
</clickhouse>
